  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nDrier air will continue to push into the area from the north \ntoday as Canadian high pressure gradually builds into the Great \nLakes, with a mix of sun and clouds expected for the remainder \nof the day. This will usher in a relatively benign stretch of \nweather across northern Michigan, with high pressure largely \nkeeping the better rain chances west and south of the area \nthrough much of the upcoming week. Northeast will be breezy at \ntimes through this afternoon/evening and again Monday as cooler \nair settles south across the Great Lakes, with Monday shaping up\nto be the coolest day of the period. Highs Monday will \ngenerally be in the mid 50s to low 60s. Cooler temperatures \ntonight will bring some potential for patchy frost across \ninterior eastern Upper, especially in areas that see lighter \nwinds. Another chilly night is expected Monday night, with \ntemperatures again falling into the 30s across many interior \nlocations bringing the potential for some patchy frost yet \nagain... Frost potential for both nights will depend on how much\nwinds diminish overnight, with the best chances occurring where\nwinds are able to decouple. \n\nTemperatures will then gradually moderate through the reminder \nof the week as the surface high slowly shifts east and weak \nreturn flow allows milder air to work back into Northern \nMichigan. Much of the week looks dry, with mostly clear to \npartly cloudy skies and seasonably cool temperatures early \nbefore the aformentioned gradual slight warmup into the 60s and \npossibly low 70s occurs by late week. Rain chances remain \nminimal through the period, with the next more meaningful \nopportunity for precipitation potentially arriving next weekend \nor early the following next week, though there is still \nconsiderable uncertainty with the timing and evolution of that \npotential at this range.",
